\description{
\code{\link[car:powerTransform]{car::powerTransform()}} estimates Box-Cox powers \eqn{\lambda = (\lambda_1, \dots,
\lambda_p)}{lambda = (lambda_1, ..., lambda_p)} that make the residuals of a
multivariate linear model as close to multivariate normal as possible. These
functions add the graphics and the workflow around it:
\itemize{
\item \code{bcProfile()} computes the profile log-likelihood for one or two of the
\eqn{\lambda}{lambda}s on a grid, and its \code{plot()} method draws it: a curve
for one variable, nested likelihood regions with the Wald confidence ellipse
for two. Optionally, Box's M statistic for the homogeneity of the group
covariance matrices (\code{\link[=boxM]{boxM()}}) is computed at the same points, so the plot
shows whether the powers that make the residuals most normal also make the
covariance matrices most alike.
\item \code{bcRefit()} refits the model with Box-Cox transformed responses, so it can
be compared with the original using \code{\link[car:Anova]{car::Anova()}}, \code{\link[=heplot]{heplot()}}, \code{\link[=cqplot]{cqplot()}},
\code{\link[=boxM]{boxM()}} and so on.
\item \code{bcBoxM()} finds the powers for which the covariance matrices of the
transformed responses are most alike across groups, by minimizing Box's M.
For a MANOVA, homogeneity of the covariance matrices is often a more
important assumption than normality.
}

This development is a new contribution to diagnostic methods for multivariate
models. It stemmed from reading Rencher & Christensen's (2012, Section 4.5)
description of the problem, and the recognition that \code{\link[car:powerTransform]{car::powerTransform()}}
had already done the heavy lifting of estimation and testing.
}
\details{
\strong{The log-likelihood.} For multivariate normality (Andrews, Gnanadesikan &
Warner, 1971) it is
\deqn{\ell(\lambda) = -\frac{n}{2} \log |\hat\Sigma_\lambda| +
  \sum_j (\lambda_j - 1) \sum_i \log y_{ij}}{l(lambda) = -(n/2) log |S(lambda)| +
  sum_j (lambda_j - 1) sum_i log y_ij}
where \eqn{\hat\Sigma_\lambda}{S(lambda)} is the ML covariance matrix of the
residuals of the model fitted to the transformed responses. Its maximum is the
estimate from \code{\link[car:powerTransform]{car::powerTransform()}}, and differences in it reproduce the
likelihood ratio tests of \code{\link[car:testTransform]{car::testTransform()}}.

\strong{Profile or slice.} With \code{type = "profile"}, at each grid point the
log-likelihood is maximized over the other \eqn{\lambda}{lambda}s, giving a
true profile, so that \eqn{2(\ell_{max} - \ell)}{2 (lmax - l)} can be referred
to a \eqn{\chi^2}{chi-square} distribution with 1 or 2 df for a confidence
interval or region. With \code{type = "slice"}, the other \eqn{\lambda}{lambda}s are
held at their estimates. This is much faster, and close to the profile when the
estimates are nearly uncorrelated (see \code{cov2cor(object$invHess)}), but it is
not a proper profile, and can mislead when they are correlated.

\strong{Box's M.} It is invariant to rescaling each variable, so no Jacobian term
is needed when it is computed for transformed responses. In \code{bcProfile()} it is
computed at the same full \eqn{\lambda}{lambda} vector as the log-likelihood at
each grid point. \code{bcBoxM()} minimizes it with \code{\link[stats:optim]{stats::optim()}} (Nelder-Mead).
Use that with care: when M changes little with one of the powers, the optimizer
can wander to extreme values of it. Plotting M against each power, with
\code{bcProfile(..., group = )}, shows where this happens. This is a multivariate
analogue of choosing a transformation from a spread-level plot
(\code{\link[car:spreadLevelPlot]{car::spreadLevelPlot()}}).

\strong{Refitting.} \code{bcRefit()} keeps the Box-Cox scaling
\eqn{(y^\lambda - 1)/\lambda}{(y^lambda - 1) / lambda} (\code{\link[car:bcPower]{car::bcPower()}}), so
the direction of an effect is preserved when \eqn{\lambda < 0}{lambda < 0}. The
transformed responses get syntactic names: a power of 1 keeps the name, 0 gives
\code{logCK}, and others \code{CK_m0.5} (for -0.5) or \code{Hpx_2}. The model is refitted with
\code{\link[stats:update]{stats::update()}} using the data frame named in the original call, with these
new columns added, so the call must have a \code{data} argument.

\strong{Positive responses only.} Only the \code{"bcPower"} family is supported, so the
responses must be positive. For responses with zeros or negative values,
\code{\link[car:powerTransform]{car::powerTransform()}} can estimate powers with other families, though these
functions do not yet handle them:
\itemize{
\item \code{family = "bcnPower"}: the Box-Cox transformation with negatives
(Hawkins & Weisberg, 2017), which estimates a location shift along with
the power (\code{\link[car:bcnPower]{car::bcnPower()}});
\item \code{family = "yjPower"}: the Yeo-Johnson (2000) transformation, defined for
all real values (\code{\link[car:yjPower]{car::yjPower()}});
\item or add a start (shift) to each response before using \code{"bcPower"}.
}
}
\examples{
data(Dystrophy)
dmd.mod <- lm(cbind(CK, Hpx, PK, LD) ~ group + age + month, data = Dystrophy)
dmd.pt <- car::powerTransform(dmd.mod)
summary(dmd.pt)

# 1-D profiles, extended to show lambda = 1, with Box's M on the right axis
op <- par(mfrow = c(2, 2), mar = c(4, 4, 4, 4))
for (v in c("CK", "Hpx", "PK", "LD")) {
  prof <- bcProfile(dmd.pt, v, extend_range = TRUE, group = Dystrophy$group)
  plot(prof, main = v)
}
par(op)
prof

\donttest{
# 2-D profile for a pair: likelihood regions and the Wald ellipse
prof2 <- bcProfile(dmd.pt, c("CK", "Hpx"))
plot(prof2)

# extended to (1, 1), with contours of Box's M
prof2x <- bcProfile(dmd.pt, c("CK", "Hpx"), extend_range = TRUE, n = 81,
                    group = Dystrophy$group)
plot(prof2x)
}

# Refit with the rounded powers (-0.5, 2, 0, 0)
dmd.bc <- bcRefit(dmd.mod, dmd.pt)
colnames(coef(dmd.bc))

# residuals before and after
op <- par(mfrow = c(1, 2))
cqplot(dmd.mod, main = "Raw")
cqplot(dmd.bc, main = "Box-Cox")
par(op)

# the transformation also makes the covariance matrices more alike
boxM(model.response(model.frame(dmd.mod)), Dystrophy$group)
boxM(model.response(model.frame(dmd.bc)), Dystrophy$group)

# Powers that minimize Box's M, vs those for multivariate normality
data(Diabetes)
Y <- Diabetes[, c("glufast", "glutest", "instest", "sspg")]
bm <- bcBoxM(Y, Diabetes$group)
round(bm$lambda, 2)
c(M_untransformed = bm$M_start, M_min = bm$M)
round(car::powerTransform(lm(as.matrix(Y) ~ group, data = Diabetes))$lambda, 2)
}
